{"parentUuid":"b7439626-70fe-4962-8c9d-1c55aef483b3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FNxiAq66ZBrPDKZ9sTB2x7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have everything needed. The tests themselves confirm the `bubbles/v2/viewport` API surface (`Width`, `Height`, `AtBottom`, `GotoBottom`, `GotoTop`, `YOffset`, `TotalLineCount`, `SetContent`, `SetContentLines`, `SetWidth`, `SetHeight`, `View`, `Update`) — if they compile and pass, the API is correct.\n\nHere is the consolidated review across the four lenses you invoked.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The committed change is a textbook *replace-hand-rolled-with-widget* refactor: the old `detailScroll` + `buildBodyLines` + `maxDetailScroll` + `clampScroll` machinery all collapses into `viewport.Model`'s own state, with `refreshDetailContent` as the single adapter that keeps content + auto-tail in sync. Switching a custom scroll calculator to a vetted widget is almost always a quality win in TUI code — fewer index-arithmetic bugs and PgUp/Home/End come \"for free\".\n- The wrap design is interesting: the **viewport receives pre-wrapped lines** via `SetContentLines(buildEventLines(buffer, width))`. That side-steps the viewport's internal soft-wrap (which doesn't know about ANSI display width) and lets `ansi.Wrap` + `sanitizeDisplayText` do width-correct wrapping. The trade-off is that every event arrival re-wraps the whole buffer — see perf note below.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n---\n\n## Scope\n\n- Branch: `feat/review-detail-viewport`\n- Commits unique vs `main`: **1** (`a9210fb9f`)\n- Files touched: `tui_text.go` (+25), `tui_detail.go` (±~250), `tui_model.go` (±~290), and their tests\n- Staged but not yet committed on top: a **separate** \"Ctrl+C double-press / explicit post-finish exit keys\" feature in `tui_model.go`/`tui_model_test.go`/`tui_sink_test.go`. I treated this as **out of scope** per your \"only the commits unique to this branch vs main\" instruction; flagging only so you know it's there. (You may want to land the viewport refactor first, then PR the cancellation work separately — those are independent concerns and the PR-review-toolkit's `feedback_few_commits_per_pr.md` memory points the same way.)\n\n## Overview\n\nThe drill-in alt-screen (`Ctrl+O`) previously rendered each event as a single truncated line, with manual `detailScroll` math choosing the visible window. This commit:\n\n1. Adds `wrapDisplayWidth` in `tui_text.go` — paragraph-aware (\\n is a break), per-paragraph sanitize, ANSI-width-correct wrap via `ansi.Wrap`.\n2. Replaces `eventLine` (string) with `eventLines` (`[]string`), and adds `buildEventLines` for the whole buffer.\n3. Replaces `detailView` (renders body itself) with `detailFrame` (wraps an already-rendered viewport body in chrome).\n4. Adds a `viewport.Model` field on `reviewTUIModel`, seeded with 80×22 defaults so a `Ctrl+O` before any `WindowSizeMsg` doesn't panic.\n5. Delegates unhandled keys in detail mode to `viewport.Update`, so PgUp/PgDn/Home/End/mouse wheel work.\n6. Preserves auto-tail via `AtBottom()` sampling around `SetContentLines`.\n\n## Code quality and style — strengths\n\n- **Correctness of the wrap behavior is well-pinned.** `TestDetailFrame_WrapsLongAssistantText` (load-bearing) verifies multi-line wrap, `TestDetailFrame_PreservesAssistantTextNewlines` pins paragraph survival, `TestDetailFrame_LinesFitTerminalWidth` and `TestDetailFrame_MultibyteRuneSafe` cover the display-width invariant. This is the right test shape.\n- **Comments are load-bearing, not noise.** The CRITICAL note about Bubble Tea's alt-screen frame diff, the AssistantText rationale in `eventLines`, the value-receiver/viewport mutation explanation on `refreshDetailContent`, and the AtBottom-driven auto-tail story all describe non-obvious *why*. Matches CLAUDE.md's guidance that comments explain hidden constraints rather than restate code.\n- **Test refactor is correct in direction.** Old tests called `detailView` in isolation; the new `renderDetailViaModel` helper drives rendering through the model with `WindowSizeMsg` + events + `Ctrl+O` + `View()`. Since the viewport is now the source of truth for body lines, exercising in isolation would have lied. Good call.\n- **Defensive defaults are reasonable.** `newReviewTUIModel` seeds 80×24 (instead of 0×0) so the first frame before `WindowSizeMsg` arrives still renders. Symmetric with the `TestTUIModel_InitializesDetailViewport` invariant.\n- **Auto-tail invariant is well-formed.** `wasAtBottom := m.detail.AtBottom()` before `SetContentLines`, then `GotoBottom` only if it was already at bottom. The `TestTUIModel_AutoFollow_PreservesUserScroll` test pins both directions (scrolled-up event = no yank; bottom event = stays at bottom).\n\n## Issues / risks (none of these are blockers)\n\n### 1. Perf: re-wrapping the entire buffer on every event arrival (medium)\n\n`handleAgentEvent` → `refreshDetailContent` → `buildEventLines(buffer, width)` re-wraps every event in the buffer every time a new event arrives for the focused agent. For a long review with thousands of events, this is **O(N²)** total work over the run.\n\n**Suggestion:** if this shows up in practice, cache wrapped lines per event (e.g. a `[][]string` parallel to `buffer`) and only wrap the appended event, plus invalidate on `WindowSizeMsg` (only width changes invalidate the cache). Not worth doing speculatively — only if a real review session feels janky.\n\n### 2. Defensive-default inconsistency between `detailFrame` and `detailViewportHeight` (low / cosmetic)\n\n- `detailFrame`: `termHeight < 3 → 3` (bodyHeight = 1)\n- `detailViewportHeight`: `termHeight - 2 < 1 → 1` (bodyHeight = 1)\n\nThey land on the same value, but via different floors. The dead branch `if bodyHeight < 0 { bodyHeight = 0 }` in `detailFrame` (`tui_detail.go:90-92`) is now unreachable thanks to the `termHeight < 3` guard above it. Either drop the dead `< 0` clamp, or unify the floors by calling `m.detailViewportHeight()` from `detailFrame`'s caller and passing it in. Minor.\n\n### 3. `detailFrame` defaults to width=80 / `detailViewportHeight` defaults to width=1 when `termWidth < 1` (low)\n\nIf `termWidth = 0` between init and the first `WindowSizeMsg` (shouldn't happen since `newReviewTUIModel` seeds 80, but still), the viewport produces 1-wide lines while `detailFrame` chrome is 80-wide. The body ends up with one rune followed by 79 padding spaces per line — wasted but not corrupted. Cosmetic; the easy fix is to make both helpers share one floor (e.g. `defaultTermWidth`).\n\n### 4. Empty `AssistantText` now contributes zero body lines, previously one blank line (low)\n\n`wrapDisplayWidth(\"\", width)` returns `nil`. So an `AssistantText{Text: \"\"}` event used to take a visible row in detail; now it takes nothing. Probably **more** correct (no point dedicating a row to a blank event), but flagging in case there's downstream UX that counted on every event being a visible row.\n\n### 5. Viewport keymap may intercept letters/keys we expect to fall through (low; verify by trying)\n\n`handleKey` delegates *anything* not matched in its switch to `viewport.Update` when in detail mode. The bubbles viewport's default keymap binds `j/k/u/d/g/G/space/b/f` (vim-style) in addition to PgUp/PgDn/Home/End. Two interactions worth a manual smoke test:\n\n- Typing `q` or `c` (without Ctrl) while in detail mode: the model has no case for these and will hand them to viewport, which is harmless (no-op).\n- The **staged** post-finish work in your worktree treats `q/Esc/Enter` as exit keys via `isPostFinishExitKey`, but `m.finished && !m.detailMode` gates them out of detail mode — so in detail mode they still fall through to viewport. Fine, but worth a manual `Ctrl+O` → `q` smoke test before merging the staged work.\n\n### 6. `splitBodyToHeight` does work the viewport may already do (low / cosmetic)\n\n`viewport.View()` should already produce `Height` lines clipped to `Width` cells (the viewport knows its dimensions). The subsequent `splitBodyToHeight` is largely there to pad short last-line content out to `termWidth` (when the viewport doesn't right-pad) and to enforce the exact-`termHeight` contract that the alt-screen frame diff needs. It's correct, but the comment on the function could call out *which* property the viewport doesn't guarantee — that's the load-bearing reason this normalization survives.\n\n## Specific suggestions (smallest first)\n\n1. **`tui_detail.go:50` — collapse the early-return in `eventLines`.** Replace:\n   ```go\n   case reviewtypes.AssistantText:\n       return wrapDisplayWidth(e.Text, maxWidth)\n   ```\n   with `raw = e.Text` and fall through to the bottom `return wrapDisplayWidth(raw, maxWidth)`. `wrapDisplayWidth` already handles embedded `\\n` for every input, so the special-casing is now redundant — the comment justifying it is true but no longer load-bearing. Saves a return path; one less branch to keep aligned if the wrap helper changes.\n\n2. **`tui_detail.go:90-92` — drop the `bodyHeight < 0` clamp.** Unreachable after the `termHeight < 3 → 3` guard.\n\n3. **`tui_model.go` — extract a `refreshAndTail()` helper.** The pattern `m = m.refreshDetailContent(); m.detail.GotoBottom()` recurs in Ctrl+O entry and Left/Right cycling. A one-line helper would dedupe three call sites and make the intent (\"switch agent, then jump to tail\") read more directly.\n\n4. **`tui_model.go:104-107` — viewport seeded height is `defaultTermHeight-2 = 22`, but model `termHeight` is `24`.** Consistent (header+footer = 2), so this is right. But consider extracting `defaultBodyHeight = defaultTermHeight - 2` as a named const so the relationship is self-documenting.\n\n## Security review\n\nThis is a pure TUI rendering change. The relevant attack surface is agent-provided text getting written to the user's terminal.\n\n- **ANSI / control-character injection from agent stdout** — addressed correctly. `sanitizeDisplayText` (used both per-paragraph inside `wrapDisplayWidth` and on event chrome strings) calls `ansi.Strip` and then drops all `unicode.IsControl` runes plus `\\r`. The `TestDetailFrame_ANSIStripped` test pins this against the cursor-hide / cursor-show CSI sequences codex emits.\n- **Width-based truncation is `ansi.StringWidth`-aware**, not byte-count. Multibyte rune safety is pinned by `TestDetailFrame_MultibyteRuneSafe`.\n- **No new I/O, no subprocess invocation, no shell escaping, no env var read/write.** No new dependencies (viewport already in `go.sum`).\n- **No data leaving the process.** The whole module is render-only.\n- **DoS surface**: A maliciously huge `AssistantText` (say 100MB) would allocate proportional memory in `wrapDisplayWidth`'s `strings.Split` and the resulting `[]string`. Mitigated only by upstream limits on agent output, not here. Probably fine for a CLI tool, but worth noting if you ever take an arbitrary attacker-controlled review feed.\n\nVerdict: **clean.** No security issues introduced.\n\n## Simplify review\n\nTwo suggestions above (collapse the AssistantText branch; drop the dead `bodyHeight < 0` clamp). Beyond that, this code is already pretty tight — the refactor *itself* is a simplification (~70 net lines deleted from the body-rendering path despite adding `wrapDisplayWidth` and viewport plumbing).\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: `eventLines` and `buildEventLines` are both exported within the package. If `buildEventLines` is the only caller of `eventLines` outside of tests, you could in-line the loop body, but `eventLines` is unit-test-friendly and the symmetry reads well. I'd keep them.\n\n## Test coverage\n\nStrong. The new tests pin every load-bearing claim in the commit message:\n\n- `TestDetailFrame_WrapsLongAssistantText` (the central promise)\n- `TestDetailFrame_PreservesAssistantTextNewlines` (paragraph semantics)\n- `TestTUIModel_InitializesDetailViewport` (no-WindowSize-yet safety)\n- `TestTUIModel_DelegatesScrollKeysToViewport` (PgDn actually reaches viewport)\n- `TestTUIModel_AutoFollow_DetailMode` + `TestTUIModel_AutoFollow_PreservesUserScroll` (both directions)\n- `TestDetailFrame_PadsToTermHeight` and `TestDetailFrame_EmptyBuffer_PadsToTermHeight` (ghost-row defense)\n\nGap I'd consider — none **blocking**, but worth a thought:\n\n- A test for the `eventLines` AssistantText empty-string case (does empty AssistantText still keep the buffer's other events visible? probably yes, since `buildEventLines` appends nothing for it, but worth pinning).\n- A test for `splitBodyToHeight` with a body that has **more** lines than `bodyHeight` — confirming overflow lines are dropped. The current test set always feeds bodies that fit, so the truncation branch is exercised implicitly via wrap tests but not directly.\n\n## Next steps you may want to take\n\n- Run `mise run check` (fmt + lint + tests) before pushing. I didn't run it; you'll want to before opening the PR.\n- Decide on the staged Ctrl+C/post-finish-exit-keys work: keep it in this PR or split out (per the few-commits-per-PR convention, splitting is the cleaner shape since the two concerns are independent).\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Ship it after the two tiny cleanups (suggestions 1 and 2) if you care to.** The refactor is well-motivated, well-tested, and the security and simplification lenses both come back clean. Net effect on the codebase is positive: less custom scroll math, better readability for multi-paragraph review findings, and PgUp/Home/End/mouse wheel now Just Work."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":102,"cache_read_input_tokens":114756,"output_tokens":5709,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":102,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"ou